<p>To enable speculative decoding for dLLMs, we must devise an attention masking strategy that enforces a <em>temporal causal structure</em> on top of the bidirectional model. This prevents future tokens from affecting past predictions, allowing us to compute valid logits for multiple drafted positions in a single forward pass.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-layout" data-code="layout">
<div class="section-no">02 — Data-Mask Layout</div>
<h2>The Duplicated Sequence Blueprint</h2>
<p>To implement this temporal context, SimSD restructures the target model's input sequence into a **paired layout**. For a speculative step with budget $\gamma$, we construct two response sections appended to the prompt:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Data Section $D$:</strong> Contains the draft model's proposed tokens (used as the reference context).</li>
  <li><strong>Mask Section $\tilde&#123;D&#125;$:</strong> Contains placeholder `[MASK]` tokens (used as the prediction slots where target logits are evaluated).</li>
</ul>

<p>Because the Data and Mask sections refer to the same logical positions in the block but sit at different indices in the concatenated sequence, standard Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) would rotate them using different index offsets. To prevent this, SimSD uses a **RoPE Copy Strategy**: each mask slot copies the position ID of its corresponding data token, aligning their position embeddings perfectly.</p>

